> i want to know what the differences are.
> i am discussing the original series, not the enhanced series.
> i believe the original au is an a series with a qlogic scsi controller.
> am i right?> 
> i use this site as a reference:
> http://h18002.www1.hp.com/alphaserver/workstations/retired/
> select a series or au series.

Yes and no.

The A series was designed for NT use only.
The AU series was designed for VMS & Unix compatibility.
There are two different basic variants of the PWS or "Miata" family.
The Miata MX5 and the Miata GL.
The Miata MX5 has no USB ports and no on-board SCSI.  The on-board SIO 
IDE chip set is not supported by VMS, but is supported by Unix and NT.
The Miata GL has USB ports and on-board SCSI and the on-board Cypress 
IDE chipset is supported by VMS.

That said, the Miata MX5 "A" series typically came with DEC branded 
Adaptec 2940UW SCSI controllers, Matrox Millennium graphics cards, no L3 
cache module, and an Toshiba IDE CD-Rom.  Some came with very high end 
Powerstorm graphics card if the system was destined to do CAD or movie 
rendering.  The Miata GL "A" variant would not need a SCSI card as it 
had the on-board version.

The "AU" series were configured slightly differently depending on the OS 
intended.  VMS destined systems could have Powerstorm or Elsa Gloria 
Synergy (permedia 2 chipset) style graphics cards, QLogic 1040UW SCSI 
controllers (if Miata MX5), 2mb L3 Cache & Toshiba SCSI CDRoms.
Unix systems were configured nearly identically except for the CDRom, 
which could be IDE or SCSI.

If I were to have a choice between a MX5 and a GL I would always go with 
the GL.

TomC



