In article <c6p33g$ehifn$1@ID-143435.news.uni-berlin.de>, "Hans Vlems" <hvlems.dotweg@zonnet.nl> writes:
:
:"jaffar" <jaffar@bluewin.ch> schreef in bericht
:news:eb198929.0404281020.79c5b39a@posting.google.com...

:> I've bought 2 AlphaStations500/266.

  Which operating system do you seek to use?  OpenVMS and Tru64 UNIX
  hobbyist programs are available, and Linux certainly functions on
  most Alpha systems. 
 
:>                                    Both have the following problem:
:>
:> If i switch the power on, the machine begins to start, but the screen
:> stays black...

  If the screen stays black, the system would appear to have problems
  with the graphics controller, or would appear to have a serial
  console enabled.  Details on switching to the serial console are
  in the OpenVMS FAQ, as well as details of the DB9 pinouts and the
  cabling, etc.

:> (One Machine was already damaged, when i got it)

  Not a good sign for any remote diagnostics of problems -- the
  remote troubleshooting of hardware can be difficult.

:> I powered the machine on, the system startet, the SMR console was on
:> the screen. I typed: run dka400.4.0.0.9.0

  With SRM, the commands you want are SHOW DEVICE and BOOT ddcu:,
  where ddcu: is a device such as DKA400:, as displayed by the
  SHOW command.  You need not add the ".4.0.0.9.0" suffix, either.

:> The CD-Rom begun to work, ACR Console was on the screen. Then, the
:> message "Read Error" was shown on the screen. The system told me to
:> power-cycle.

  ACR?  Do you mean the ARC console?

:> After i pushed the reset switch, the Screen staied black.
:>
:> What can i do, to re-get the SMR?

  It is interesting that you got from SRM to ARC, if that is what
  has happened.

:Instead of run you should have used boot:
:
:>>> b -fl a dka400                            for booting Tru64 unix
:>>> b -fl 0,0 dka400                         for VMS

  Those are SRM console commands, and the poster might be running the
  ARC console.  (The other command needed for those two boot commands
  is the "setenv os_type <name>" SRM console command, to prepare the
  SRM firmware to run the UNIX or the OpenVMS operating system.)

  To switch from the ARC console (also known as AlphaBIOS) to the SRM
  console, see the sequence in the OpenVMS FAQ.  Pointers to details
  of upgrading SRM firmware are also available via the FAQ.


