In article <k54g4c.mp3.ln@escape.goid.lan>,
Charles Shannon Hendrix  <cshSPAM@SPAM.widomaker.com> wrote:
>In comp.sys.dec, you wrote:
>
>The conversion electronics must not be compatible with my newer
>drives.  
>
>The original drives were 7200 rpm Seagate Barracudas, and I assume these
>RZ sleds just don't like much else. One sled is narrow, two are wide
>internally.
>

Due to some deficiency in their capability to predict the future, DEC
designed the narrow SCSI backplanes in such a way that not-yet-designed
wide SCSI SBB's would not work in them.

A partial recovery from this allows older narrow SCSI SBB's to work
in wide SCSI backplanes.  The BA353 has a narrow backplane

>It's too bad, because I know where there are quite a few BA3xx pizza
>boxes and towers.  But if their RZ sleds won't mate with newer drives,
>they aren't much use.

With regard to heat and power considerations, you should look at the
specifications of the new drives.  There has been a considerable
decrease in drive power consumption over the past decade or two.
Having 40GB per platter instead of 100MB per platter reduces the
number of platters per drive and hence the air friction pumping loss.
Reduced the mass of the head assembly also lowers the power
consumption during seek activity.

