No BA353 here, but my docs concur that only 5400RPM drives should be 
used in that case.  We had one that a customer stuck three RZ29-L drives 
in that lasted about 3 weeks; the plastic was discolored on the outside 
and 'toasty brown' on the inside, and the SBBs were even worse, before 
the power supply failed.

I have a BA364 case here with two 9GB Ultra-7200RPM drives in it; the 
DS-RZ1DB-VW died last year and I was able to find a 50GB replacement for 
it that had slightly lower power draw; same Seagate series, but a newer 
high-capacity version.  Model ST150176L but it reports as a SX150176LC, 
and uses an SCA connector.  Its been working for months in the same SBB 
cannister that held the old 9GB drive, and lights and ID work as 
expected.  Definitely a cheap and useful upgrade for my hobby system.

