The InfoServer 100 was based on a MV3100 Model 20 foundation. The firmware
was changed. The Infoserver 150 was a value engineered design that more
closely reflected it's funcitnal use, thus it was a firmware change and the
floating point unit was left off the module. In both Infoservers the OS was
dedictaed to serving up blocks of data, via, LAD (local Area Disk) and LAST
(Local Area System Transport) and MOP. Both these systems copied their OS
from a CD-ROM to an internal disk then from that point onwards they booted
from the internal disk. The machines could serve blocks of data to anything
that had a driver to speak to the Infoserver, thus PC, Macintosh, VXT ,
ULTRIX, OSF, VMS and other things like DECServers that did MOP loading could
all get data from one of these machines. VMS Standalone Backup could be
booted from one of these onto a VMS system and of course the VMS machines
could install Layered Product being served on the network by a Infoserver.
The InfoServer 1000 was a value engineered product as well and did away with
the internal hard disk in favour of programmable memory. They were built
into small tower units with multiple CD-ROM's so that you could load them up
with Layered Product distribution CD's.
The 150 broght both internal SCSI buses to the outside world to maximize
expandibility. There was alos a Tape Server protocol available but I do not
remember anything about this.
Somewhere around here I have the design spec for LAD, LAST and possibly LAT
and MOP. These describe the protocols at the bit level in the ethernet
packets from what I remember, quite detailed.

rtt




