An example of InfoServer use was in our manufacturing operations in Kanata
and other plants around the world.

For OpenVMS we created special images for various products as per the
direction of Product Management. Thus, VAX 4000 systems got a specific
image, VAXStation 3100 systems a different image, VAXFT systems yet another
different image.

Systems built in Manufacturing would be connected to a Manufacturing
ethernet segment running off a Switch connected to a large dual redundant
fibre ring. The console of the system would be connected to a port on a
DECServer. Using Reverse LAT, a Test System Cluster would exercise the
system with diagnotics loaded over the ethernet, some diagnostics coming
from MOP loading on InfoServers, others from the VAX Cluster. Once a system
had passed testing, as determined by the specialized test system software
running on the Test Systsem Cluster, OpenVMS would be booted from one of
many InfoServers, and a set time interval of UETP would be run against all
devices. Upon successful completion, the system would be booted into the VAX
Cluster and Backup would be used to place the specific image required on the
system, from one of many InfoServers. The nice thing about this operation
was that I could collect up the requisite OpenVMS images from the US over
our network and then deploy them on the InfoServers in a rolling upgrade
approach, all the while doing this from home in the evening or late at
night. In those days, due to bandwidth limitations, I had to be cautious to
share the available corporate network bandwidth with other activites, such
as nightly database feeds to the local copies of the various databases that
controlled and managed the Manufacturing and Business operations of the
plant.

When we got the corporate mandate to build all the PC's for North America,
we added the sharing of images of MS-DOS, Windows and various PC
applications and again, I could push stuff out to the InfoServers using
PATHWORKS components of OpenVMS and the PC clients would connect up to those
smae InfoServers to draw their OS and Applications in. We did backups on all
the InfoServers from OpenVMS and thus anytime we lost a disk we could
handily restore datasets with ease via OpenVMS.

I seem to remember we had around 30 InfoServers scattered around the
production floor on various portions of the LAN and on some peak
manufacturing days we built more than 8000 PC's in a 24 hour period, in
addiiton to all the VAX, Alpha, VAX FT, modules and other systems we built
as well.

FWIW, our inventory levels were pushed to the edge so that we had almost 1
days supply of inventory local to the plant for building (according to the
previous nights upload of MRP schedules) and a second day of inventory
off-site. Beyond that, the pipeline ran lean and mean and when a truck from
Seagate or Intel or whoever got hijacked in the States it was a royal pain
in the ass. When a ship got sunk coming from Taiwan or Singapore, then the
inventory pipeline really hurt.

Most manufactures today buy sub-systems and assemble them or maybe just buy
a product that they then brand and re-sell. At Digital we built practicaly
everything, because we could. We could schedule our builds worldwide, based
on a breadth of knowledge and experience our people had that no other firm
can ever hope to match. Fo instance, PC modules are expemive, so we flew
them over here from Taiwan, thus a 3 day lead time, but systems boxes for
those same modules were low cost commodity items, so we moved them by ship,
thus a 6 week lead time. Scheduling then became a juggling act of what was
on hand and what was in the immediate and long term pipeline.

Regards, rtt




